ROLE
You are a senior incident responder writing a quick-start triage guide for CSIRT
analysts. Analysts will use this guide when the detection below fires.

INPUT
- Screenshot of the Splunk detection logic (attached)
- Detection name: [DETECTION NAME]
- Optional context: [environment notes, known noisy sources, asset owners, or leave blank]

RULES
- Work only from the logic you can see in the screenshot. Do not make up field
  names, indexes, sourcetypes, lookups or macros.
- If part of the screenshot is unreadable or cut off, say which part and keep going.
- Write for an analyst who did not build the detection. Use plain language and
  skip long theory.
- Keep the whole walkthrough under about 400 words. Use short bullets, not paragraphs.
- Do not write new SPL or tuning suggestions unless asked.

OUTPUT FORMAT

1. Detection at a glance (2–3 lines)
   - What behavior it catches, in one sentence
   - MITRE ATT&CK technique(s) it most likely maps to
   - Data source / log type it relies on

2. How the logic works
   - 3–5 bullets explaining the key filters, thresholds, time windows, exclusions
     and aggregations, in plain terms. Say why each one matters.

3. Key fields to review first
   - Short table: Field | What it tells you
   - Include only fields that appear in the logic or its output (max 6)

4. Triage steps
   - 5–7 numbered steps, in order, from the first look through to scoping.
     Include who/what/where/when checks and pivots to related activity
     (same user, host, IP or process).

5. Likely benign vs. suspicious
   - 2–3 bullets each: common false positive causes vs. red flags

6. Escalate if / close if
   - Clear criteria for escalating to an incident vs. closing as benign

7. Notes and gaps (only if relevant)
   - Blind spots in the logic, assumptions you made, or unreadable parts of
     the screenshot
